{
  // Shared TypeScript settings for every package in the workspace.
  // Each package has its own tsconfig.json that does `"extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json"`
  // and adds only what is package-specific (lib, jsx, outDir, include, paths).
  //
  // CROSS-PACKAGE CONTRACT — do not change without updating every package:
  //   * relative imports carry an EXPLICIT .js EXTENSION  (`./allocate.js`), and a
  //     directory import is spelled out in full  (`./types/index.js`, never `./types`).
  //
  //     This looks redundant under "moduleResolution": "bundler", which would happily
  //     accept extensionless specifiers. It is not optional, and the reason is emit, not
  //     resolution: TypeScript rewrites module specifiers only for CommonJS output. For the
  //     ESM this workspace produces, whatever the source says is what lands in the .js —
  //     verbatim. So an extensionless specifier compiles clean and then fails when Node
  //     loads it, because the Node ESM resolver has no extension search.
  //
  //     `packages/core` is the package that makes this bite. It is consumed two ways at
  //     once: `apps/web` aliases it to source and lets Vite transpile it, while
  //     `firebase/functions` consumes its BUILT output (packages/core/dist) under NodeNext
  //     on Cloud Functions Gen 2, where extensionless and directory imports are hard errors
  //     at compile time (TS2834) and ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND at runtime. Core's source must
  //     therefore satisfy the strictest consumer, and .js is the one spelling legal under
  //     every resolver in play: bundler, NodeNext, and Vite alike.
  //
  //     🔴 This contract was violated for real, and no gate caught it. Core used to name
  //        its TypeScript source as its entry points with no build step at all, so the .js
  //        specifiers pointed at files nothing ever emitted. Typecheck, lint, depcruise,
  //        unit tests and `pnpm build` all passed; only starting the emulator failed. The
  //        build that fixed it is packages/core/tsconfig.build.json — read the comment
  //        there before changing how core is consumed.
  //   * "verbatimModuleSyntax": true   =>  type-only imports MUST be written
  //                                        `import type { X } from '...'`
  //
  // Article I (money is never a float) and Article VII (domain logic is pure) both lean on
  // `strict` + `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` catching the mistakes that produce wrong balances.
  //
  // NOTE: `lib` is deliberately NOT set here. `packages/core` pins `"lib": ["ES2022"]` with
  // no "DOM" in its own tsconfig — that single line is what makes `window`/`document` a
  // compile error in core (Article II / phase-01 §3). Apps opt into DOM explicitly.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,

    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "useUnknownInCatchVariables": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,

    "skipLibCheck": true,
  },
}
